DOCUMENT();
############# # Load Macros #############
loadMacros( "PGstandard.pl", "PGML.pl", "AnswerFormatHelp.pl", "PGlateximage.pl", "PCCmacros.pl", "contextInequalitySetBuilder.pl", "parserGraphTool.pl", "ORCCA.pl", "PGcourse.pl", ); COMMENT('Authored in PreTeXt');
####### # Setup #######
$a = random(-7, 7); InequalitySetBuilder::UseVerticalSuchThat(); Context("InequalitySetBuilder-Only"); $set_builder = Compute("{ x | x <= $a}"); Context("Interval"); $interval = Compute("(-inf, $a]"); $gt = GraphTool("{interval,$interval}")->with( availableTools => ['IntervalTool', 'IncludeExcludePointTool'], numberLine => 1, xAxisLabel => 'x', bBox => [ -8, 8 ], ticksDistanceX => 1, minorTicksX => 0, useBracketEnds => 1, showInStatic => 0, showCoordinateHints => 0, );
$image_1 = createLaTeXImage(); $image_1->addToPreamble(latexImagePreamble()); $image_1->BEGIN_LATEX_IMAGE \begin{tikzpicture} \begin{axis} [ numberline, xmin=-8, xmax=8, xlabel={\(x\)}, width=0.47\orccaprintwidth, ] \addplot[infiniteclosedinterval] coordinates {(-12,0) ($a,0)}; \end{axis} \end{tikzpicture}
END_LATEX_IMAGE
###### # Body ######
BEGIN_PGML [`[$set_builder]`]
[_]{$gt}{1}
[@KeyboardInstructions('In interval notation:')@]**
[_]{$interval}{12}
END_PGML
########## # Solution ##########
BEGIN_PGML_SOLUTION The set-builder notation tells us to graph all numbers less than or equal to [`[$a]\text{.}`] So the shading can start all the way to the left "at [`-\infty`]" and extend up to [`[$a]\text{.}`] There should be a bracket at [`[$a]`] to indicate that [`[$a]`] is included.
>> [@image(insertGraph($image_1), width=>600)@]* <<
And we read the number line from left to right, with the shading extending to [`-\infty`] and going all the way up to [`[$a]\text{,}`] including [`[$a]\text{.}`] So in interval notation, the interval is [`[$interval]\text{.}`]
END_PGML_SOLUTION
